  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1237 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nIt is scorching hot again today across the quad-state. Noontime\ntemperatures are in general about one to two degrees warmer than\nyesterday at 96-98 at Poplar Bluff to Mt. Vernon and 93-96 east\nof there. Low 70s dewpoints are providing heat index values\naround 105. Tomorrow and Friday look very similar with deep\nlayer ridging from the southeast to the central Plains only\namplifying, if anything. Lows only drop into the mid 70s, which\nwill continue to produce another extended period of dangerously\nhot conditions.\n\nBy Saturday broadly cyclonic flow over eastern Canada bows\nsouthward in a way that produces more northwesterly flow aloft\nand also sends a backdoor \"cold\" front towards the region from\nthe northeast. LCL heights are very high as this front comes\nthrough due to deep mixing with poor mid level lapse rates with\n500mb heights still sitting at 590-592 dm and ensemble guidance\nis handicapping our PoPs pretty low presumably as a result. The\nchance of a little rain over the Saturday-Sunday period is\n 30-50% but in any one 6 or 12 hour time period the chance \nappears pretty small/difficult to pin down. The front looks to \ndrop temperatures a few degrees, although heat index values will\nstill likely be pushing 100 Sunday-Tuesday. Upper level ridging\nsnaps right back into place early next week and we look likely \nto continue a hot and mostly dry pattern into the middle of next\nweek. Ensemble temperatures are a little cooler, in the lower \nto mid 90s, but I wouldn't be shocked to see those start to \ntrend up given the 598+ dm 500mb heights and full sunshine.",
